---
description: Add a brief comment on new code explaining why it exists and what it does
alwaysApply: true
---

# Comment why and what

Comments guide developers reading the code. When adding or changing code, write **at least one line** on every class, interface, enum, record, service, use case, and method (JavaDoc `/** ... */` for public ones), plus non-obvious blocks:

- **What** it does (the behavior)
- **Why** it is there, when not obvious (the reason / problem it solves)

Keep it short (1 line, up to 3 for methods). Do not narrate every line inside a method. Keep existing user comments. Getters, record accessors, and trivial one-line delegations need no comment.

This rule wins over any other rule that says "explain why, not what".
